## INTRODUCTION
Post-translational modification of proteins is involved in almost all life processes, and which plays an important regulatory role for the function and activities of proteins.1 Among them, protein glycosylation and phosphorylation are the most common post-translational modifications, since the proteins are central in understanding numerous biological events.  Glycosylation sites, as biomarkers and therapeutic targets, is closely involved in the occurrence and development of many diseases such as the tumors, the inflammation, and the Parkinson's disease.2 Recent studies have shown that the abnormal levels of phosphoproteins is closely related to the pathogenesis of major diseases.3,4 However, is there a certain correlation between the levels of glycosylation and phosphorylation of proteins in biological event? Although the previous works have been involved, there is no clear conclusion. In different cases, the levels of glycosylation and phosphorylation may be synergistic improvement or mutually inhibited.5-7 The research on the relationship between the levels of glycosylation and phosphorylation of proteins is considered to be an effective way for understanding the occurrence and development of major diseases, which is of great significance for studying mechanisms and early diagnosis of the disease.
At present, the levels of protein glycosylation and phosphorylation are usually identified by immunoblotting, affinity chromatography, $\boldsymbol{^{32}\mathrm{P}}$ radiolabeling, mass spectrometry, and so on.8-10 Among them, bio-mass spectrometry has been widely used in research of protein phosphorylation and glycosylation.11-14 However, to realize in situ and dynamic analysis of protein phosphorylation and glycosylation in cells and in vivo, the mass spectrometry still has certain difficulties. As powerful techniques, fluorescence methods has the advantages of high sensitivity and wide dynamic response range due to the special photophysical and photochemical characteristics of the fluorescent materials.15-18 More importantly, real-time fluorescence imaging of the levels of protein phosphorylation and glycosylation can be achieved by laser confocal imaging. Fluorescence methods combined with confocal microscopy and micro-spectral detection technology can achieve detection and imaging of the levels of protein phosphorylation and glycosylation in cells and in vivo.
Currently, the specific recognition and imaging analysis of post-translational modification of proteins in cells and in vivo by fluorescence method is still poorly developed.19,20 And in situ fluorescence imaging of protein phosphorylation and glycosylation sites in cells and in vivo has not been reported so far. To achieve specific recognition of the levels of protein phosphorylation and glycosylation, the required fluorescent probes must have functional units that specifically recognize protein glycosylation and phosphorylation sites and also highly sensitive signal transduction unit. Nanomaterials have significant advantages to build such type of functional probes. Specific recognition units and fluorescent materials can be assembled by self-assembly to construct multifunctional fluorescent nanoprobe, which able to realize in situ fluorescence imaging of protein glycosylation and phosphorylation in cells and in vivo.
In recent years, MOFs, as a new type of porous coordination material,21-31 has been widely used in biosensing, catalysis, photodynamic therapy due to its controllable synthesis, structural diversity and high specific surface area.32-37 Recently, we used $\mathrm{Cu(II)}$ as the active center of nano-MOFs to effectively reduce the concentration of GSH, synergistically increase the concentration of ROS, accelerate the apoptosis and increase the death, strengthen the photodynamic therapy effect, and achieve synergistic antitumor.38 We used $Z\mathrm{r}(\mathrm{IV}).$ - based nano-MOFs that is well suited for specific adsorption and selectively phosphate’s fluorescence sensing, directly decreasing the blood phosphorus levels and lead to hyperphosphatemia’s remission.39 Can we combined a metal ion that is easy to bind phosphorus with an active group that is easy to interact glycosyl group, and thus design and assemble a novel fluorescent nanoprobe that is expected to achieve simultaneous detection of the levels of glycosylation and phosphorylation. Herein, based on the above strategy, a nanoMOFs material with $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ and boric acid as the active center was designed and prepared. According to the specific interaction between $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ and phosphate, combined the fluorescence intensity off-on characteristics, and fluorescence imaging of phosphorylation site was realized. With aim to achieve specific recognition of glycosylation sites, the boronic acid group was modified in the MOFs structure, and the ligand fluorescence was regulated by the alizarin red. Thus, fluorescence imaging of glycosylation sites was achieved through the competition between alizarin red and glycosyl. Subsequently, depressed mice model was established and we verified the mice model of depression through behavioral test. Finally, the relationship between the levels of glycosylation and phosphorylation in depressed mice was studied by in vivo imaging and combined with two-photon imaging. The results showed that the levels of glycosylation and phosphorylation sites in depressed mice model were significantly lower than that of the normal mice. This study provides a suitable fluorescent tool for further reveal the relationship between the levels of glycosylation and phosphorylation and its correlation with mechanism of the disease.
