## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Materials. $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ was purchased from Bellingwell (Beijing, China) and 2-aminoterephthalic acid was purchased from Shanghai Siyu (Shanghai, China). Alizarin red S, D-Fructose and D-Galactose were purchased from Macklin (Shanghai, China). 3-Carboxyphenyl boronic acid, 4-(2-hydroxyethyl)-1- piperazineethanesulfonic acid (HEPES) and Neuraminidase were purchased from aladdin (Shanghai, China). L-cysteine Acid, glutathione and MTT were purchased from SigmaAldrich (Shanghai, China). Other related inorganic compounds: D- $\cdot(+)$ -glucose anhydrous ${\mathrm{K}}_{3}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ , ${\mathrm{NaHCO}}_{3}$ , ${\mathrm{NaHSO}}_{3}$ , NaCl and etc. were purchased from Sinopharm (Shanghai, China). Analytical grades of all other chemical reagents were used directly without further purification. Ultrapure water $\left(18.2\mathrm{M}\Omega\cdot\mathrm{cm}\right)$ was obtained from a Water Pro water purification system (Labconco Corp., Kansas City, MO)
Instruments. All one-photon fluorescence measurements were measured at room temperature on an F-4600 fluorescence spectrometer. Transmission electron micrographs and scanning electron micrographs were obtained using a HITACHI HT7700 electron microscope and a SUPRA 55 electron microscope, respectively. Powder X-ray diffraction patterns were performed at room temperature on a D8 ADVANCE X-ray powder diffractometer. UV-visible absorption was measured on ThermoFisher EVOLUTION 220 UV-Vis spectrophotometer. Dynamic light scattering (DLS) and Zeta potentials of the MOF material were taken on a Malvern instruments Nano-ZS90. The two-photon excited fluorescence spectra were measured using a Zeiss LSM 880 NLO, and the two-photon images were acquired with the Zeiss $\mathrm{LSM}880\mathrm{NLO}$ with a $20\times$ water objective.
Synthesis of the UIO- ${\bf66-N H}_{2}$ . $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ $\mathrm{0.082g}$ , $0.35~\mathrm{mml}$ ), 2- aminoterephthalic acid $(0.0635\ \mathrm{g},\ 0.35\ \mathrm{mmol})$ was dissolved in a mixture of $10~\mathrm{ml}$ of DMF, $1.5\mathrm{ml}$ of acetic acid and $0.1\mathrm{ml}$ of deionized water at room temperature. After ultrasound to complete dissolution, the mixture was stirred for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . it was transferred to a $20\mathrm{\ml}$ polytetraethylene-lined stainless steel reaction vessel and reacted in an oven at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 24 hours. After the reaction was cooled to room temperature, it was centrifuged $(8000\mathrm{r/min})$ , and the lower layer precipitate was collected and washed three times with DMF. The washed product was thoroughly dispersed in $25\mathrm{\ml}$ of DMF and immersed for 24 hours. After centrifugal, and then thoroughly dispersed again in $10~\mathrm{ml}$ of ethanol solution for 24 hours, the product was again centrifuged, and the product was dried in a vacuum oven to obtain a product.
Synthesis of the MOFs nanoprobe. Alizarin red $\left(0.1593\ \mathrm{g}\right.$ $0.46~\mathrm{\mmol}_{,}$ ) was dissolved in $1~\mathrm{ml}$ of DMSO at room temperature, and added dropwise to CPBA (0.2738 g, 1.64 mmol) dissolved in $1.0~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMSO and $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of Hepes buffer $\mathrm{(pH=8.0)}$ . After sonicating for 5 minutes, it was shaken in a $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ constant-temperature shaking incubator for 2 hours, and UIO-66 $(0.1449\ \mathrm{g})$ was added, and then shaken in a constant-temperature shaking incubator at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 24 hours. With another centrifugation $(8000\mathrm{r/min})$ , and the product is repeatedly cleaned several times with DMF, $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and ethanol. Then the product was dried in a vacuum drying oven to obtain a product.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Characterization.The general scheme for preparation of the MOFs-based nanoprobe is illustrated in Scheme 1. In this work, alizarin red and phenylboronic acid first form composites through the specific interaction of cis-diol with boric acid. Then the complex is embedded in the cavity of the MOFs and thus formed the nanoprobe by self-assembly of composites and UIO-66 MOFs. Since the cis-diol in the sugar molecule has a stronger binding ability to boric acid than that of the boric acid and the alizarin red complex, so the boric acid and the alizarin red complex are dissociated through competition, and thus the fluorescence detection of the target is realized by fluorescence intensity off-on. Through the specific binding of $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ and phosphate, the fluorescence of MOFs ligand is regulated, and the specific detection of phosphate is realized. Simultaneous recognition of glycosylation and phosphorylation sites can be achieved by using the as-prepared nanoprobe, which provides a suitable fluorescence tools for revealing the mechanism of disease by studying the interaction between glycosylation and phosphorylation sites in disease.
We first prepared the $Z\mathrm{r}(\mathrm{IV})$ -based nano-MOFs probe and characterized the crystal structure and morphology of MOFs probe by PXRD, IR, SEM and TEM. PXRD characterizes the crystal form of the MOFs probe (Figure 1a). There was no significant change in the crystal form of the MOFs material before and after the modification, which due to the crystal structure of MOFs material is mainly determined by $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ and ligand. The mixture of alizarin red and 3- Carboxybenzeneboronic acid is only embedded in the void of MOFs, but has little effect on the crystal form of MOFs material. The view is further confirmed by the FTIR spectrum and the UV-vis absorption spectrum of the material that the modified MOFs has no obvious influence on the crystal form of the MOFs material (Figure S1), but the Zeta potential of the modified MOFs is reduced from $24~\mathrm{mV}$ to $-23\ \mathrm{mV}$ . We used DLS, SEM and TEM to characterize the morphology of the material. The MOFs material has a particle size of about 120 nm (Figure 1b, 1c, 1d).
Fluorescence response. The recognition performance of the MOFs-based nanoprobe was investigated according to fluorescence response of the nanoprobe with different concentration of saccharide (Figure 2). As shown in Figure 2, the fluorescence intensity of the MOFs-based nanoprobe was increased gradually with the concentration of the saccharide increased. By interacting with saccharide, the alizarin red is released due to the interruption of charge transfer between alizarin red and boric acid, which causes the fluorescence of alizarin red off-on. The performance of three saccharide reacted with the probe were tested and found that the fluorescence can be enhanced, which further illustrate the above mechanism (Figure 2a-c). In addition, after the addition of the saccharide, the color of the solution changed from orange-red to purple-red (Figure S4), which enables colorimetric analysis of the saccharide. By interacting with the phosphate, the charge transfer between the ligand molecule and the metal node of MOFs is interrupted, so the fluorescence of the ligand is restored (Figure 2d), and thus realizing the fluorescence detection of phosphate.
Selectivity. The specificity of the MOFs-based nanoprobe was further investigated. Active substances and various ions were selected as interference to test the selectivity of the nanoprobe (Figure S5). It can be seen that phosphate and the D-Fructose can significantly enhance the fluorescence intensity of MOFs probe, while interfering substances have little effect on MOFs fluorescence. The results indicated that MOFs probe has a good selectivity for the phosphate and saccharide, respectively. Before performing bioimaging, we tested the standard 3-(4,5- dimethylthiazol-2-yl)-2,5-diphenyltetrazolium bromide (MTT) assay and the results showed that MOFs probe have low cytotoxicity (Figure S7). We then used the laser confocal imaging to explore the intracellular level of glycosylation using fluorescence imaging (Figure S6). As expected, bright fluorescence was clearly observed in HL-7702 cells and SMMC-7721 after $3\ \mathrm{~h~}$ , which showed the distribution of glycosylation sites. To prove the specificity of glycosylation sites imaging, we incubated the cells with sialic hydrolase that removes sialic acid from the cells. After $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ , fluorescence was clearly observed. It is worth noting that its fluorescence intensity is weaker than the cells incubated with MOFs alone. Therefore, we infer that nanoprobes can specifically recognize glycosylation sites in cells.
Fluorescence imaging of depressed mice. Next, the MOFsbased nanoprobe was applied for in situ fluorescence imaging the levels of glycosylation and phosphorylation in depressed mice. With aim to reveal the relationship between the levels of glycosylation and phosphorylation in the brain of depressed mice, we established a chronic unpredictable mild stress mice model of stress depression. Then, according to the behavioral test (Figure S8), the sucrose preference of the mice with depression was significantly reduced, and the immobility time of the mice with depression in the forced swimming test and tail suspension test was significantly longer, the above behavior proves that the mice depression model has been successfully established. Based on behavioral test, we divided them into susceptible and resilient groups. As shown in Figure 3, the fluorescence images intensity in the brain of susceptible mice was significantly reduced compared to the normal group of mice. At the same time, the fluorescence images intensity of the mice with resilient was significantly higher than that of the susceptible mice. More importantly, the same results were observed (Figure 4) through two-photon fluorescence imaging. Results of both imaging indicated that significant decrease in glycosylation and phosphorylation levels in depressed mice, which mainly due to a disorder of metabolic levels depressed mice. In addition, the level of glycosylation in the brain of depressed mice is further confirmed by in vivo imaging system (Figure 5). In conclusion, the above results exhibited that the level of glycosylation and phosphorylation in the brain of depressed mice is decreased than that of the normal mice, which indicated that the level of glycosylation and phosphorylation are mutually inhibited in depressed mice. In general, this works providing new fluorescent tool for the study of the mechanism of depression.
CONCLUSIONS
To explore the relationship between levels of glycosylation and phosphorylation, we developed a nano-MOF nanoprobe with $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ and boric acid as the active center, and realized the specific recognition of phosphorylation levels by using its fluorescence response. In order to achieve specific recognition of glycosylation sites, the boric acid group was modified in the MOF structure, the glycosylation was detected through the competition between alizarin red and glycosyl. The relationship between glycosylation and phosphorylation in depressed mice was studied by in vivo imaging combined with two-photon imaging, which showed that the glycosylation and phosphorylation levels in the brain of depressed mice model were significantly lower than that of the normal mice. The present work provides new ideas for reveal the mechanism of disease through study of the interaction between glycosylation and phosphorylation.
